      "beats": [
        {
          "say": "A dot product combines matching coordinates. For unit-length vectors, it equals cosine similarity and measures directional alignment. Normalizing removes vector magnitude from that comparison, making the angle between two representations the quantity being scored in this particular metric.",
          "start": 0.4,
          "end": 18.304,
          "duration": 17.904,
          "until": 18.904
        },
        {
          "say": "Follow the river and water arrows. They point in similar directions in our constructed map, while money points elsewhere. Bank begins between the two groups. These relationships were chosen for the demonstration; a trained model’s actual neighbors need to be measured from its vectors.",
          "start": 18.904,
          "end": 35.296,
          "duration": 16.392,
          "until": 35.896
        },
        {
          "say": "Coordinate systems matter. Two embedding models can both output the same number of dimensions while arranging information differently. Comparing one model’s vector with another model’s vector using an ordinary dot product requires an established alignment or shared training contract; matching dimensions alone leaves that relationship unestablished.",
          "start": 35.896,
          "end": 56.152,
          "duration": 20.256,
          "until": 56.952
        }
      ],
